@situation

= The situation in October 2019

PiS has won a second term. The Left has returned to the Sejm with 12.56% and
49 seats on the SLD electoral committee after a four-year parliamentary
absence. The common delegation brings together SLD, Wiosna, Razem and smaller
left organisations.

The chapter starts after the election, passes through the July 2021 merger
revolt, and ends with the July 2023 election-readiness report.

- @menu: Back

@factions

= Organisations and political caucuses

**Organisational ancestry** and **political tendency** are separate.

1. SLD provides the legal vehicle, regional machinery and many experienced
officeholders.
2. Wiosna provides Biedroń's network and a strongly progressive public brand.
3. Razem remains a separate party with activists and a costed social programme.
4. Labour, feminist and younger progressive currents cross those formal lines.

The “barons” are the old SLD elite: organisationally experienced, socially more
cautious and economically accommodationist. The younger progressive caucus is
more women-led, secular, feminist and LGBT+ supportive. Katarzyna Kotula is
Wiosna-derived even when she aligns with that functional caucus.

- @menu: Back

@institutions

= Institutions and parties

The president can sign, veto or refer ordinary legislation and compete for
credit. He does not choose routine parliamentary cabinets as Hindenburg could
in the inherited scenario.

PSL and the later Poland 2050 are coalition hinges, not equivalents of the
Weimar Centre Party. PiS, Konfederacja and the Braun current are distinct
competitors. Razem is not the KPD. The Polish Armed Forces and police are
modelled through professionalism, legality, readiness and public trust—not
partisan loyalty or militia strength.

- @menu: Back

@senate

= Sejm, Senate and confidence

The Senate is a separate chamber, not a decorative seat total. On an ordinary
bill it may accept, amend or reject the Sejm text; the Sejm can override that
position with an absolute majority. On a budget it has twenty days and may
propose amendments, but it cannot reject the whole act.

The 2019 opposition pact begins with 52 nominal senators but only 51 reliably
deliverable votes. Lewica has just two senators, so influence comes from keeping
KO, PSL and independents coordinated, preparing amendments and becoming the
vote that neither larger camp can take for granted.

Cabinet formation is decided in the Sejm. The President nominates the first
prime-ministerial candidate. If that cabinet fails, the Sejm may choose a
candidate. A later constructive vote of no confidence must name a replacement
and assemble 231 deputies; the Senate does not participate.

- @menu: Back

@economy

= Two different currencies

**Political resources** represent party cash, organisers, staff capacity,
volunteers and usable relationships. They are generally uncapped. The alliance
collects its annual income once when January begins; there is no automatic
monthly refund. Annual income combines membership dues with a subvention
calculated from the seats won at the last Sejm election. Raising dues brings an
immediate receipt and a larger January collection, but burdens the party's
least affluent base. Emergency appeals share a long cooldown even when a
different treasurer or adviser makes the call.

**Budget capacity** represents a government's room to fund additional promises.
It is also uncapped and may be negative after an overcommitted settlement.
Opposition parties do not control it. Their annual budget game normally ends in
a vote against the government, a costed alternative, or a deliberately costly
issue deal. A governing coalition sets the next year's capacity through its
internal, coalition, Sejm and Senate budget stages.

- @menu: Back

@polling

= Polls and voters

Eight Polish electorate blocs sum to the country: metropolitan liberals,
liberal professionals, public-service families, industrial and logistics
voters, provincial welfare voters, rural localists, older welfare households
and anti-establishment youth.

Every party competes inside every bloc. Positions, organisation, credibility,
turnout and campaign actions produce vote intention, a sampled headline poll
and a national d'Hondt indicator.

PiS, KO, PSL, Konfederacja and Poland 2050 spend separate organisational pools
in the background. Their pairwise relations stay hidden; the ledger reports
only coalition compatibility and projected seats.

- @menu: Back

@rules

= Prototype rules

Each month:

1. Draw from Party Affairs; from Government Affairs when Lewica holds a
relevant ministry; or from Negotiate with Government when Lewica is outside a
functioning cabinet. All three feed one persistent three-card agenda.
2. Play one card and choose its political resolution. Playing the card spends
the month; resources return only through choices and the annual income event.
3. Open one of the three active advisor portraits. All advisor interventions
share a six-month cooldown.
4. Use the leadership-bureau card to edit the three-seat slate through the five
political caucuses. A changed slate locks for six months; an unchanged
inspection does not. Or deliberately **End month** without acting and accept
the neglect penalty.
5. Resolve the dated national event.

The separate 2023 route adds election results, coalition arrangements,
confidence arithmetic, ministry allocation and the first coalition budget.
The overview and monthly event desk always name the cabinet, prime minister,
president and Lewica's actual role. Supporting one opposition amendment never
silently turns Lewica into a governing party.

